<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grigory Shelikhov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1747">1747</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grigory Shelikhov was a Russian merchant and fur trader who founded the first permanent Russian settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, at Three Saints Bay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak Island</a>, in 1784. His company, merged with rival firms after his death, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>, chartered by Paul I in 1799 to govern Russian America.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov.jpg" alt="Grigory Shelikhov" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Wikispork at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grigory_Shelikov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grigory Ivanovich Shelikhov was born in 1747 in Rylsk, in the Belgorod Governorate, the son of Ivan Shelikhov. He had a sister, Agrofena, and a younger brother, Vasilii, who went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> with him to help with the business. In 1775 he married Natalia Alexeyevna Kozhevina, whose family belonged to a prominent clan of Okhotsk navigators and mapmakers. At his death he had five surviving daughters and one son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1775 Shelikhov organised voyages of merchant ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuril-islands">Kuril Islands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands">Aleutian Islands</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. In 1783 he founded the Shelikhov-Golikov Company in partnership with Ivan Larionovich Golikov, and in 1783 to 1786 he led an expedition to the coast of the mainland, where his party founded the first permanent Russian settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he hired Alexandr Baranov in 1790 to manage his fur-trading enterprise in Russian America.</p><p>In April 1784 he arrived at the place he named Three Saints Bay, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak Island</a>, with two ships, the Three Hierarchs and the St. Simon. The Koniaga, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Alutiiq</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, defended themselves at Refuge Rock on Sitkalidak Island. In what became known as the Awa'uq Massacre, Shelikhov's armed men, who had guns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, killed hundreds of the Alutiiq, including women and children, and took hundreds of hostages, many of them children, to force other Alaska Natives to submit. Having established his authority on Kodiak, he founded the settlement at Three Saints Bay. Unalaska had been settled long before, but it was not regarded as a permanent Russian base until his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shelikhov died on 31 July 1795 (20 July in the old style). In 1799 the assets of his company were merged with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a> under its correspondent Nikolai Rezanov, who was Shelikhov's son-in-law, having married his fourteen-year-old daughter Anna in January 1795. A gulf in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-okhotsk">Sea of Okhotsk</a>, a strait between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak Island</a>, a bay on Kruzof Island and a town in Irkutsk Oblast bear his name, and a statue of him stands in Rylsk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grigory Shelikhov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Vitus Bering's voyage of 1741, Siberian traders hunted sea otters in the Aleutian Islands, and <b>Grigory Shelikhov</b> founded a permanent settlement on Kodiak Island in 1784.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rylsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.57,34.68&amp;year=1778" aria-label="Rylsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5334 379 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Grigory Shelikhov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5784 649h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5784 649h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.57,34.68&amp;year=1778">What was here in 1778? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
